#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6b3754-c8d5-5b37-8d18-ef83f5e9749c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238185} {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238401} {:answer/id #uuid "366bc6b5-4bab-5ba8-8018-c4bc7223b115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238525} {:answer/id #uuid "69582467-05df-57a2-b597-530abbc3877d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238970} {:answer/id #uuid "93d981b6-c7f1-52e4-a22e-60dd625357db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e14937-1c82-559e-a37e-1b482fa437f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238401} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17fe9cc-10a6-5ca0-8dc9-5ed726aaaab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c05cb83d-c779-50b1-a772-7d4551eea239" :question/math-academy-id "q-105308" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-16{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -12 & -16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d87b39f-38fd-5072-8df9-199dc3e0354b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239142} {:answer/id #uuid "9363b93b-17ff-5622-aad6-e074a8b8cff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239327} {:answer/id #uuid "9a31168b-c0d9-57f7-abbb-5a6d79071e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239395} {:answer/id #uuid "de1e8102-fb34-585a-b310-16891b8d4acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239996} {:answer/id #uuid "e66fc050-4e7a-5846-932f-1446097346fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1e8102-fb34-585a-b310-16891b8d4acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239996} :answer-field/id #uuid "2020bb5b-ccfc-53a2-812c-aa951625fd94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e39d7a95-6796-5061-a2aa-706f3f881251" :question/math-academy-id "q-105315" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -5 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+2(0)+5(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f4c6f1-939a-5b88-9bf3-a8adf2ea2c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238698} {:answer/id #uuid "572c908c-7588-5745-b1fb-06f0deaa26f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238807} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef98273-4408-5085-9187-33c5ddd46ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239443} {:answer/id #uuid "c13e9a64-4df3-5744-803e-cb874d004f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239746} {:answer/id #uuid "fa77997b-23f4-5dcb-b0af-e29180f4d714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef98273-4408-5085-9187-33c5ddd46ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f9d469-87fe-56b1-97f4-7fe1b4596a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81d16fc8-2819-579f-80e6-748f22f14098" :question/math-academy-id "q-105319" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -15 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+4(0)-4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ea8503-592e-5da9-bf94-dbf1c1894619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238691} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3cb585-f127-5930-9e3b-7bcca744902f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238718} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239510} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eef8cf-fdfa-56b9-be1c-3b3a679c443b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239949} {:answer/id #uuid "e723e2bb-2b91-51b6-b191-0ff5b0c1e575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7b5c69f-96d8-52e0-a849-ceb99bf9c83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239510} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b2343f-c078-5f49-8bf2-efe62f09b44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2c7d13-747e-5ac0-bf88-b6f7f16d61aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105334" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n\nII. The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/a1/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/02/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "438dc9b9-4b4c-5c7c-8f1f-ef06b777ccee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238640} {:answer/id #uuid "863a21f0-1bc2-56d2-9c36-a6fabc7dd44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239223} {:answer/id #uuid "990a09cd-f697-5ab6-845b-769bca59df37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239385} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf99d1d-2ca4-5a96-93f3-6bba5cba0073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239849} {:answer/id #uuid "dafd1bdc-8b8a-5ac7-a122-fcacd9a19c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafd1bdc-8b8a-5ac7-a122-fcacd9a19c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239965} :answer-field/id #uuid "954844d7-9a40-5243-995c-752e01be8ec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26f835f8-28cf-54e5-acfe-863ecc606ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-105357" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(3,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/b9/b9b92085d112ab87592aa8c4d6a56aa4a61ea859d560bac316ce8c97a0af20e5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,0)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27806c9bb67b60d52cfbae8e881d307d71a3a5fb9465f36d978035f74d78f4bc.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220d3b6e-16be-5d03-8218-f5badf84607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238352} {:answer/id #uuid "38f9139a-fc27-571a-b253-0ced64754412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238547} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea25008-a389-52eb-b71d-5d28d07da799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239294} {:answer/id #uuid "953f2088-4959-5da7-870c-162bf4221448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239347} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9414f6-0aee-551b-8d5f-6308c06b3b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f9139a-fc27-571a-b253-0ced64754412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238547} :answer-field/id #uuid "e850dda3-900d-5f07-872b-367021ae60ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f57db6a6-9edb-5f0f-98dd-62f31492deff" :question/math-academy-id "q-105365" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/21/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e40bb52-d447-5b63-8524-b2a531405e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241441} {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bc1ea3-bf67-5bbc-afec-5c54acd87a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242768} {:answer/id #uuid "d10a3eb1-dd7e-54ff-a306-219702a84f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243022} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0d8c7b-86b5-51b3-a866-ce768aef7b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242466} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd08eacf-dccc-58c1-bd33-7fea6c93187f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0880be83-dbd5-5dc8-a3cc-bdb045c80795" :question/math-academy-id "q-106684" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The  zero-matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $L.$\n- For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e801e2-503a-559b-936f-f3458cb4c980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241902} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0376d8-916a-5c20-b820-b920ea81d4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242150} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242932} {:answer/id #uuid "e35537bf-65b9-5ffa-a98b-f238d2931250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243206} {:answer/id #uuid "f976c12a-1c69-5418-8d74-80ce87edd353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a62f5fb8-4988-5219-9bbb-89bef4000d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2555255-85b1-529f-85fb-94b6f9684401" :question/math-academy-id "q-106688" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n\nII. For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n\nIII. There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487a4843-7c15-5ab1-8920-ea735dfdde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241837} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242005} {:answer/id #uuid "70a81903-8370-5b92-9e82-aeee09fbedcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242186} {:answer/id #uuid "8832c7a4-ead6-55c1-ba06-ea22126f602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242375} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa223a8-d4f3-51ef-a918-0edfc6559919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242005} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6e7b59-87d4-567b-a3cf-d17339b0c4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f184570-b064-50c3-a82a-5d76cef8f98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106700" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241426} {:answer/id #uuid "68cb4930-5346-5250-b195-1320dc59351d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242121} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3cfbd3-7c51-51e1-a1cc-651adea01a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242288} {:answer/id #uuid "a3041743-ae01-5749-b3bd-e63042670543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242605} {:answer/id #uuid "af66c2ef-de66-52df-845a-a3736459f400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241426} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0d854e-3563-5ab5-a40f-3c0263e3318d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c35f03a5-0abf-5f24-a015-92c06eed08bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106703" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7041d5fd-aab1-57e4-9487-b5c9882af600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242180} {:answer/id #uuid "72850430-5595-545e-a2f2-2771edf7ffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242195} {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242335} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe59f91-6410-5a6d-8e3e-2fcdb03f1f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242826} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa01516-17a5-5f65-a695-9c94fa04b033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242335} :answer-field/id #uuid "4127109b-95ff-5b82-9a34-cadc07c4058a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c34661b-de46-5e42-88de-7fe1ed369bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106710" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241327} {:answer/id #uuid "36663d6f-568a-5128-b12e-c1c00efc0541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241659} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3d6cce-e8b2-553a-80b1-192aefaf4bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241862} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd26119-c084-57ff-a50a-31cfe1d896f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241876} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d83713-39f9-5e61-ab77-6113890a9500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241327} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8abf082-e05d-5b29-89ee-0c3cab9c4c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d83ac51c-0c75-5956-b55e-b6c1972a1661" :question/math-academy-id "q-106809" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aea2174-dfeb-5a96-8044-c2efb4e92930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241278} {:answer/id #uuid "1f09035c-a73f-5bc0-b1e9-62489ebb423b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241452} {:answer/id #uuid "86070dcd-5d3e-5df7-96bd-50a77e905437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242352} {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243016} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5f3220-69a4-5a66-9699-c599ac5f66f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243016} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d3769e-1942-5ecd-9ab4-4be4b985cc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "253cf041-a7dc-53ad-a439-778e84d95095" :question/math-academy-id "q-106810" :question/problem "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n\nII. For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03039dc6-1dfb-5fb8-9cd9-a75ee2ded12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241207} {:answer/id #uuid "054b883f-bba5-5e0c-a4b6-5ae661f7f6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241224} {:answer/id #uuid "1082defe-5e72-57fe-9385-7ab66130f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241322} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bf2a8-2d06-5f33-a19e-4f65b98a806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242967} {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229} :answer-field/id #uuid "54287edb-3a24-5a97-817d-0ce7b6fd42c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43cc36f0-0278-53e6-b346-3f4ef69a0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-106812" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e27a77-17e4-5b05-b8b5-42ddb50a3663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241473} {:answer/id #uuid "338251e4-d90f-50e0-b7e1-23d7e28826c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241634} {:answer/id #uuid "467f0cad-0f65-5c2a-a7dd-e55f70fc3b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241817} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242016} {:answer/id #uuid "f4501265-2c93-55db-a331-1380629939f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242016} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9c9993-a272-54fc-b7cd-ff51bde6faba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d584308b-6723-5e86-a9ec-6146a097d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107042" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n- ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{4}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{3}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{4}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution, which contradicts the fact that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{3}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242157} {:answer/id #uuid "8e744672-b586-517b-b695-2c0349dfe2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242432} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfe95b-b76c-5d99-948c-1a376b43ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242698} {:answer/id #uuid "b9613222-8a5b-5a7a-b79e-e341b2f7bdab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242805} {:answer/id #uuid "d41f0180-5fdd-5302-b5d0-55961dd132a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242157} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55f8088-4d40-5543-b0fb-123e13d3f497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ec52379-6b69-5ec0-af4a-1a5b49cab942" :question/math-academy-id "q-107043" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+1\\cdot {v}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
